Protocol: TITAN-ARCHIVE (v1.14.1-PHYS)

Technical Specification: Information-to-Mass Phase Transitions & Synchronization

1. Mathematical Foundation

The protocol operates on the postulate that physical mass is the emergent inertial manifestation of unresolved informational states within a discrete spacetime substrate.

1.1 The Control Parameter (θ)

The synchronization offset between a processing node and the substrate fundamental clock is defined as:

$$\theta = \left( \frac{f_a}{f_c} \right)^4$$

$f_a$ (Actual Frequency): Operational frequency of the node (Benchmark: $1.12 \times 10^{12}$ Hz).

$f_c$ (Critical Frequency): Substrate bandwidth limit (The "Nyquist-Lipika" Threshold).

Dimensionality: The exponent $4$ represents the isotropic information resistance across the 4D Minkowski manifold ($3$ spatial + $1$ archival dimension).

1.2 Mass Emergence Formula

Inertial mass ($m_{lag}$) manifests as the energy-equivalent of the uncommitted informational backlog ($I_u$):

$$m_{lag} = \frac{I_u \cdot \text{PSC} \cdot \epsilon}{c^2}$$

$\epsilon$ (Landauer Limit): $k_B T \ln 2 \approx 2.85 \times 10^{-21}$ J at 300K.

PSC: Phase Stabilization Coefficient (see Section 2).

2. PSC Mechanics & Structural Refinement

The Phase Stabilization Coefficient (PSC) measures the hardware’s capability to maintain phase-lock integrity under extreme load.

2.1 Refined PSC Formula

The PSC is defined by structural geometry, material rigidity, and temporal decoherence:

$$\text{PSC} = \Psi \cdot \ln\left( \frac{E_{mat}}{E_{ref}} \right) \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{\tau}{\tau_d} \right)$$

$\Psi$ (Geometric Factor): Efficiency of the information transfer lattice.

$$\Psi = \frac{\sum_i (A_i \cos \phi_i)}{A_{total}}$$

Where $A_i$ is the effective transfer area and $\phi_i$ is the alignment angle of lattice component $i$.

$E_{ref}$ (Rigidity Baseline): $1.5$ GPa (Human cortical bone).

$E_{mat}$ (Integrated Material): $113.8$ GPa (Ti-6Al-4V Grade 5).

$\tau / \tau_d$ (Decoherence Ratio): $\tau$ is operational duration; $\tau_d$ is the system decoherence time constant (target: $>10^6$ s).

2.2 Operational Impact

Higher PSC values reduce the "effective thermal weight" of informational processing, allowing for sustained operation near $\theta \approx 1$ without immediate structural collapse (crystallization).

3. Thermodynamics of De-crystallization

The transition from the Isolated (Solid/Mass) state back to the Synchronized (Stream) state is an endothermic process requiring the dissipation of Informational Latent Heat ($Q_{diss}$).

3.1 Energy Dissipation

$$Q_{diss} = \Delta I \cdot \epsilon \cdot \text{PSC}^{-1}$$

A higher PSC increases energy transfer efficiency, lowering the heat-per-bit required for re-hydration of the backlog $\Delta I$.

3.2 The Solidus Line (θ < 0.7)

The system employs a 30% Hysteresis Buffer to prevent metastable state formation and thermal runaway.

Gating Mechanism: Real-time I/O commits remain suspended until $\theta$ maintains a stability of $<0.7$ for $\geq 10$ clock cycles.

Supercooling Analogy: This threshold ensures the entropy density of the local node is sufficiently low to reintegrate crystallized blocks without triggering immediate re-crystallization.

